// §249 — TAGALOG (Filipino). Keyed by the English source (normalized innerHTML
// for rich blocks, text for plate labels); see src/explain-i18n.js for why.
//
// Register: standard Filipino as written in software and instructional web
// content. Tagalog grammar throughout; the established English technical loans
// stay in English spelling (escapement, balance, pallet fork, hairspring,
// mainspring, fusee, pinion, torque) because a Filipino reader of a watch
// explainer meets them only that way, and generic things take their ordinary
// native word (gulong, ngipin, pingga, kadena, martilyo, kamay ng orasan,
// makina ng relo). One movement cannot have two names for a part: the glossary
// is shared with the chrome (src/i18n.js) and the primer.
//
// A loan stem takes a separate linker «na» and never a fused «-ng», and a
// borrowed verb carries its affix after a hyphen (i-wind, naka-pose).
//
// NUMBERS AND <code> ARE NOT TRANSLATED — they are the source constants this
// page exists to quote. Emphasis the English carries as CAPITALS rides the
// wording, never an added tag. "modelado" vs "simulado" tracks the repo's
// modelled/simulated distinction exactly (modelado = inilalarawan, simulado =
// PINAGAGALAW); HONESTY.fil gates it, and the bare noun «modelo» is
// deliberately not the honesty word.

